# How this deck was made (W3C PROV-O): researched card by card from the sources below,
# each card checked against at least two of them, then in the review rounds below.
<#compilation>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T12:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:used <https://api.nobelprize.org/2.1/> ,
              <https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q44585> ,
              <https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/lists/all-nobel-prizes-in-chemistry/> ,
              <https://sv.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nobelpriset_i_kemi> ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith <#anton-wiklund> ;
    prov:wasInformedBy <#review-0> ,
                       <#review-1> ,
                       <#review-2> ,
                       <#review-3> ,
                       <#review-4> ,
                       <#review-5> ,
                       <#review-6> ;
    rdfs:comment "Compiled by Anton Wiklund with the help of AI."@en ,
                 "Sammanställd av Anton Wiklund med hjälp av AI."@sv ,
                 "About this record: the comments on this compilation, its review rounds and its sources are condensed from the deck's dossier, the working record kept while the deck was compiled, from which a provenance report was generated. The dossier's per-card evidence and Wikidata checks, its numbered queries and scripts (its Queries section) and its logs of discrepancies are not part of this release; where the text refers to them, to the dossier or the report, or to findings listed below, it means that record. The builder is the script that built the deck from the dossier and ran its Wikidata checks."@en ,
                 "The cards were checked by machine (live Wikidata and the app's SHACL and DCAT-AP validators)."@en ,
                 "Prizes and selection. On 2026-10-04 the Nobel Prize API was queried for all Nobel Prizes in Chemistry (nobelPrizeCategory=che, sorted by year; see Queries) and for all chemistry laureates. It returned 125 prize years, 1901–2025, and 198 laureates (two of them with two chemistry prizes, so 200 laureate-years). Eight years have no laureates: 1916, 1917, 1919, 1924, 1933, 1940, 1941 and 1942; NobelPrize.org's summary page for each of them says \"No Nobel Prize was awarded this year\". They are left out. Each of the other 117 years became one card, with the year (the API's awardYear, the year the prize is for) on the front, tagged zxx as a number in no language."@en ,
                 "Back: English. The laureates of each year, in the order the API lists them, joined with \", \" and \" and \". The English names are the API's knownName.en, the names under which the Nobel Foundation lists the laureates (titles such as Sir and Lord included: Sir William Ramsay, Lord Todd), unchanged except in two places: the API's \"Aleksey Yekimov \" has a trailing space, removed; and \"Jaroslav Heyrovsky\" is written \"Jaroslav Heyrovský\", his Czech name and Wikidata's English label (as the peace deck restored the accents of María Corina Machado and Óscar Arias Sánchez)."@en ,
                 "Back: Swedish. The same laureates joined with \", \" and \" och \". Each Swedish name is the API's knownName.se (from the laureates endpoint, see Queries; the nobelPrizes endpoint has English names only) unless Swedish has an established form that differs, taken from Wikidata's Swedish label (query 2) and confirmed by the Swedish Wikipedia's table of laureates (its link targets): Nikolaj Semjonov (API \"Nikolaj Semjenov\"), Ilya Prigogine (API \"Ilja Prigozjin\", a transliteration from Russian of the name of a Belgian chemist, who wrote it Prigogine) and Jaroslav Heyrovský (API \"Jaroslav Heyrovsky\"). Every other Swedish name is the API's, which for these laureates is the same as the English name."@en ,
                 "Notes. Every card has a backNote saying briefly what the prize was for, written in the deck's own words from the API's English motivation (motivation.en) and, for the Swedish note, the API's Swedish motivation (motivation.se) where it is given and correct; the notes shorten the motivations, sometimes keeping their wording (for example the Swedish notes for 1987 and 2013); the API is CC0, so this needs no attribution. Where the laureates of one year were cited for different work, the note names each (for example 1912: \"Grignard: … Sabatier: …\"). Four notes add a fact about another Nobel Prize, each from the API's laureate records and Wikidata's P166: Marie Curie's earlier Physics Prize (1911), Linus Pauling's Peace Prize (1954), and Frederick Sanger's and K. Barry Sharpless's second chemistry prizes (1958/1980, 2001/2022). The API has no Swedish motivation for 2021 and a wrong one for 1958 (see Quality control); the Swedish note for 2021 was translated for this deck, its term confirmed by the Swedish press release on NobelPrize.org, and for 1958 it was translated from the English motivation. Swedish vitamin names follow the form \"C-vitamin\" throughout."@en ,
                 "Second source for every laureate. Wikidata was queried (query 1) for every item with P166 award received = Q44585 (Nobel Prize in Chemistry) and the year of its P585 point in time qualifier. It returned 200 laureate-years for 198 items. All 198 laureates in the API were matched by the Wikidata id the API itself gives (laureate.wikidata.id), and every one has Q44585 with exactly the API's year or years; Wikidata names no holder of the prize that the API does not."@en ,
                 "Third source. NobelPrize.org's summary page for each year 1901–2025 was fetched (one request per second, script fetch_summary.py) and compared with the cards: every laureate's name (the API's knownName or fullName) appears on that year's page, the eight years without an award say \"No Nobel Prize was awarded this year\", and the nine prizes whose award decision the API dates to the following year (1914, 1918, 1920, 1921, 1925, 1927, 1938, 1943 and 1944) each say the laureate \"received his Nobel Prize one year later\". NobelPrize.org is copyrighted and used for verification only."@en ,
                 "Machine checks. Every card carried Wikidata checks run by the builder against live Wikidata, 548 in all: for every laureate, P166 includes Q44585; the English and the Swedish name on the card is a Wikidata label or alias in that language, or the language-independent \"mul\" label or alias, where Wikidata has that form (each card's Wikidata evidence says which: 20 names on 14 cards are only mul forms, Marie Curie having no English or Swedish label at all); and Q7186 (Marie Curie) P166 Q38104 and Q48983 (Linus Pauling) P166 Q35637 for the notes. The checks read only truthy property values and cannot see the P585 year qualifier, so the link between year and laureate — the core fact of each card — is verified by query 1 (re-run on 2026-10-04 with the query \"P166 years\" in the dossier's Queries: every card's P166-checked items are exactly the items whose Q44585 statement has that card's year), not on every build; it should be re-run before release. Name checks are left out where the card's form is not on Wikidata in that language (mostly the Nobel Foundation's forms with initials or titles, such as \"Francis W. Aston\" or \"Lord Todd\"); every such name was confirmed against the API and the NobelPrize.org summary page, and the Swedish ones also against the Swedish Wikipedia table; they are listed in Quality control. The deck was then validated with the app's SHACL and DCAT-AP validators (node scripts/validate_sources.ts nobel-prize-in-chemistry)."@en ,
                 "Study direction. Front to back only: Frederick Sanger and K. Barry Sharpless each appear on two cards, and the backs, lists of names, are not fit to be asked in reverse."@en ,
                 "Reproduction. The cards were generated from the saved API and Wikidata responses by a script (gen_cards.py) that applies the name decisions in steps 3 and 4 (Back: English and Back: Swedish) and no other transformation (every other name is the API's), joins the names, adds the notes (notes.py, written by hand), writes the evidence of each card from the saved responses and NobelPrize.org pages, and adds the Wikidata checks; a second script (assemble.py) wrote the dossier. The scripts (gen_cards.py, notes.py, compare.py, svlist.py, fetch_summary.py, assemble.py, runq.sh) and all raw responses are kept in a scratch directory outside the repository, so the generation step itself cannot be rerun from the repository; the queries and URLs below are enough to fetch the same data again and the rules above to rebuild the cards."@en ,
                 "Selection: All 117 years from 1901 to 2025 in which the Nobel Prize in Chemistry was awarded, one card per year, with every laureate of that year: the complete set, nothing chosen. Left out: the eight years with no award (1916, 1917, 1919, 1924, 1933, 1940, 1941 and 1942), since a card for them would have no laureate to learn; and the 2026 prize, which NobelPrize.org says is announced on 7 October 2026, after this deck was made on 4 October 2026. The year is the prize year, not the year it was awarded: the nine prizes reserved and awarded the following year (1914, 1918, 1920, 1921, 1925, 1927, 1938, 1943 and 1944) are on the card of the year they are for (Fritz Haber's 1918 prize, awarded in 1919, is on the card 1918). The notes say what each prize was for and, where a laureate won another Nobel Prize, which; they do not give the laureates' countries or affiliations (several laureates had more than one), and they do not mention that Richard Kuhn (1938) and Adolf Butenandt (1939) were made by the German authorities to decline their prizes and later received the diploma and medal: the API, the content source, records both prizes as received, and NobelPrize.org, which tells the story (for Kuhn), is a verification source only."@en ,
                 "Licensing: The deck is CC0 1.0. Its content comes from two sources whose terms allow that: the Nobel Prize API, whose terms say the service is \"free to use according to the Creative Commons Zero (CC0) license\", and Wikidata, whose structured data is CC0. The selection (every prize year with an award), the laureates and their order, the English and most Swedish names, the facts in the notes and the motivations the notes paraphrase come from the API, cross-checked with Wikidata; the three Swedish forms and one English spelling that differ from the API come from Wikidata's labels. The notes are in the deck's own words. Two sources were used for verification only and contributed nothing but confirmation: NobelPrize.org's pages (copyright Nobel Prize Outreach: \"You may not reproduce, distribute ... or otherwise use the Content without prior written permission\"), read to confirm what the open sources give — the evidence quotes each year's one-sentence award statement from that year's summary page with the motivations elided, and the title and five-word motivation of the 2021 Swedish press release (short quotations, to show what was checked; the same facts are CC0 from the same maker through the API, and the quotes are in the provenance report, not on the cards), but no text, selection or data field was taken, which also keeps clear of the EU sui generis database right (Directive 96/9/EC) in the site as a whole; and the Swedish Wikipedia's table of laureates (CC BY-SA 4.0), compared with the Swedish names but not copied, its selection not used (copying the table would have made the deck CC BY-SA). The API terms say that, in case of conflict, they prevail over CC0, and they add conditions for applications: not to alter the data or mislead users — the deck changes the API's names in five documented places only (in English a trailing space removed and an accent restored; in Swedish three established forms of names: Semjonov, Prigogine and Heyrovský), and its notes summarise the official motivations without changing their meaning — and to credit the API as the data source, which the deck does. They also say applications may not use Nobel Prize Outreach's name or trademarks \"in their titles or logos, or in any way that implies endorsement\", and NobelPrize.org's terms of use say \"you may not use the Nobel Foundation trademarks without prior written permission from the Nobel Foundation\". The deck's title, \"Nobel Prize in Chemistry\" / \"Nobelpriset i kemi\", uses the prize's name only to say what the deck is about (descriptive use: there is no other way to name the subject), with no logo, medal or other Nobel graphics, and its description says it is not affiliated with or endorsed by the Nobel Foundation or the Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences. As for the literature and peace decks, whether that satisfies the API terms' \"titles\" clause is an open question; the fallback is a title without the prize's name, such as \"Chemistry laureates 1901–2025\" / \"Kemipristagare 1901–2025\"."@en .

<#review-0>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Completeness and correctness against the sources; agreement between the Nobel Prize API, Wikidata, NobelPrize.org and the Swedish Wikipedia; Wikidata machine checks"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 117 cards."@en ,
                 "Outcome: The API's 125 prize years (117 with an award, 8 without) and 198 laureates (200 laureate-years) were matched one by one against Wikidata's P166 Q44585 statements with their P585 years and against NobelPrize.org's summary page for every year. The laureates and years agree in all three, with no exception; the eight years without an award and the nine prizes awarded a year later agree between the API and NobelPrize.org. All 548 Wikidata checks passed in the build (2026-10-04). No review round has been run yet. The P166 checks confirm that each laureate received the prize but cannot see the year; the year of each card rests on the API, NobelPrize.org and query 1 (the P585 years), as method step 8 (Machine checks) says."@en .

<#review-1>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Factual accuracy"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 117 cards, the sources and the method."@en ,
                 "Outcome: The review re-verified all 117 cards against the Nobel Prize API, Wikidata (P166 with P585) and NobelPrize.org, fetched independently, and found no factual error on any card; the documented name changes, the nine reserved prizes, the eight years without an award and the 2026 cut-off were confirmed. One documentation error (which API endpoint gives the Swedish names) was fixed; the suggestion on year-blind P166 checks was answered by re-running the year check and recording it."@en .

<#review-2>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Language, translation and language tags"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 117 cards, the title, description and keywords, in both languages."@en ,
                 "Outcome: Language tags are correct throughout (zxx fronts, en and sv backs and notes, no untagged text); Swedish names and terms follow the API's Swedish motivations, Wikidata and established forms. No errors or warnings; of three suggestions, two Swedish/English note wordings were applied and the capitalisation of Sir/Lord/Jr. in Swedish was kept, as a library-wide question."@en .

<#review-3>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Licensing, attribution and documentation"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 117 cards, the sources, licence evidence, method, queries and licensing."@en ,
                 "Outcome: Licensing found sound: both content sources are CC0, every licence quote verbatim, nothing from the two verification sources on the cards, and every quoted API motivation, NobelPrize.org sentence and Swedish Wikipedia row confirmed. One error: the Wikidata evidence called 20 names on 14 cards \"en/sv label or alias\" although Wikidata has them only as language-independent (mul) labels or aliases. Fixed by restating every Wikidata name in the evidence by its exact kind (label, alias, mul label or mul alias), on all cards with name checks. The endpoint suggestion was the same as round 1's and is fixed; the remaining \"authoring agent\" wording in the method and licensing was replaced."@en .

<#review-4>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Re-check of changed cards and a sample (facts, language, documentation)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 117 cards re-compared by script with the Nobel Prize API and Wikidata (fetched 2026-10-04), NobelPrize.org summary pages for a sample of cards, the 2026 announcement date, the rounds 1–3 fixes, description, keywords, topics, method and licensing."@en ,
                 "Outcome: No errors or warnings: every back, note, P166 and label check, and every quoted evidence string matched the live sources, the 2026 cut-off holds (chemistry prize announced 7 October 2026), and the fixes of rounds 1–3 are present. Two optional wording suggestions, both confirmed and applied: the Swedish description now lower-cases \"nobelpris\" used as a common noun, and method step 5 no longer claims that the notes never quote the motivations."@en .

<#review-5>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Final full-deck review (facts and language)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 117 cards against the live Nobel Prize API (fetched 2026-10-04): backs, name order, notes against the English and (sampled) Swedish motivations, Swedish language and name forms, language tags, description, keywords, study direction, method and licensing, and the round-4 fixes."@en ,
                 "Outcome: All 117 cards match the API apart from the documented name changes; the 2026 cut-off holds and the round-4 fixes are present. One warning, confirmed and fixed: the licensing text miscounted the departures from the API's names. One optional suggestion, applied: the 1957 note now gives Lord Todd's personal name and when he took the title."@en .

<#review-6>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-10T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Version 2: title, description and keywords in Korean, German, Spanish and French (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: the deck’s title, description and keywords, translated by an AI agent from the English (the Swedish settling what was ambiguous) into Korean, German, Spanish and French, in the words the app itself uses, and checked by a second AI agent that translated every text back into English and fixed what had drifted; the release compared by a script with version 1, which found the new title, description and keywords and the release’s own metadata the only changes, and checked by the library checker; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: the title, description and keywords are in Korean, German, Spanish and French as well; no card changed."@en .

<https://api.nobelprize.org/2.1/>
    dcterms:title "Nobel Prize API 2.1 (api.nobelprize.org): Nobel Prizes in Chemistry and chemistry laureates (nobelPrizes and laureates endpoints)" ;
    dcterms:creator "Nobel Prize Outreach" ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/> ;
    rdfs:comment "Every API response's meta block links the terms (\"terms\": \"https://www.nobelprize.org/about/terms-of-use-for-api-nobelprize-org-and-data-nobelprize-org/\", \"license\": \"…#licence\"), which say: \"The Services are free to use according to the Creative Commons Zero (CC0) license. In case of conflicting terms or other inconsistencies, these Terms shall prevail.\" They add: \"Applications that use the Services should not deliberately alter or censor the data, or deliberately mislead the end users\" and \"Applications and other products and services may not use Nobel Prize Outreach’s name, logos, or other trademarks in their titles or logos, or in any way that implies endorsement, sponsorship, or false association with Nobel Prize Outreach or any other association within the Nobel Foundation’s sphere. However, you are encouraged to cite the Services as the data source for your application.\""@en .

<https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q44585>
    dcterms:title "Wikidata" ;
    dcterms:creator "Wikidata contributors" ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/> ;
    rdfs:comment "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Wikidata:Licensing: \"All structured data (i.e. the main, Property, Lexeme, and EntitySchema namespaces) is released into the public domain under Creative Commons Zero.\""@en .

<https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/lists/all-nobel-prizes-in-chemistry/>
